---
import InstrumentBand from "../../../components/lab/instrument/InstrumentBand.astro";
import FieldComparison from "../../../components/studies/stellar-encounters/FieldComparison.svelte";
import { encounters } from "../../../components/studies/stellar-encounters/fields";
import {
  flyEncounter,
  gravityReach,
  poweredBand,
  startingFlight,
} from "../../../components/studies/stellar-encounters/flight";
import MotionComparison from "../../../components/studies/stellar-encounters/MotionComparison.svelte";
import { studies } from "../../../data/studies";
import StudyLayout from "../../../layouts/StudyLayout.astro";

const study = studies.find(
  (entry) => entry.slug === "stellar-encounters-2026-11-11",
);
const reference = encounters.map((encounter) => ({
  encounter,
  band: poweredBand(encounter),
  runs: flyEncounter(encounter, startingFlight),
}));
---

<StudyLayout study={study}>
  <section>
    <h2>01 / A new star asks for a different approach</h2>
    <p>
      Approaching an unfamiliar star should invite recognition and a decision:
      what opportunity does this place offer, what threatens the ship, and how
      should the pilot approach or escape? The implemented standard Sun is the
      fixed reference: collect energy while managing gravitational pull,
      exposure and terminal contact. This study defines a small initial set of
      additional stellar encounter identities, each changing a pilot decision in
      a way its appearance and behaviour help communicate.
    </p>
    <p>
      Real stellar relationships guide the design; distances, timescales and
      visual differences are compressed for readable arcade play. The
      <a href="/lab/studies/suns-2026-11-07#stellar-model">earlier Sun study</a>
      distinguishes temperature and colour, size, mass, radiant output and
      activity. Those are investigation dimensions, not a requirement to vary
      everything or adopt its proposed encounters. The tension is between
      coherent stellar character and differences that matter in flight. Our
      starting hypothesis is that stars offer different combinations of
      opportunity and danger rather than only an ascending difficulty ladder.
    </p>
    <p>
      The families, their number, their encounter behaviour and their readable
      cues remain open. Compare them with the same ship, fitting, starting
      resources and resource protection rules, using production flight systems
      to observe approach, collection and escape; a colour comparison alone
      cannot establish a different challenge. The standard Sun’s mechanics and
      current ship handling remain fixed. Future travel between systems could
      make these identities meaningful destinations; travel, destination
      selection and progression are adjacent questions outside this cycle.
    </p>
    <aside class="question-check" aria-label="Question test result">
      <p>
        Question test · 11 November 2026: One subject — Pass; Grounded — Pass
        with findings; Consequence — Pass. The comparison controls were
        clarified after independent review; the corrected opening was not
        retested.
        <a href="/lab/reviews/stellar-encounters-2026-11-11"
          >Full result and responses</a
        >
      </p>
    </aside>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2 id="spatial-identities">
      02.1 / Two orange stars, two different approaches
    </h2>
    <p>
      The first proposal contrasts a quiet K dwarf with a cool evolved giant,
      against the standard Sun. Both new encounters can look orange. The dwarf
      asks for a close approach to earn a modest refill; the giant offers
      collection farther out but occupies much more space. These are proposed
      encounter characters, not an accepted roster or difficulty ranking.
    </p>
    <h3>What the science gives us</h3>
    <p>
      Colour describes temperature, not total output or stellar life stage. K
      dwarfs are cooler and less luminous than the Sun. An evolved star can
      instead expand into a cool, luminous giant. That gives us two different
      spatial situations without inventing a colour-coded danger scale.
      <a
        href="https://science.nasa.gov/missions/hubble/goldilocks-stars-are-best-places-to-look-for-life/"
        >NASA: K dwarfs</a
      >;
      <a
        href="https://science.nasa.gov/missions/hubble/hubble-witnesses-the-final-blaze-of-glory-of-sun-like-stars/"
        >NASA: cool, luminous giants</a
      >. “Giant” describes an evolved structure, not an extra letter after the
      O–B–A–F–G–K–M temperature sequence.
    </p>
    <p>
      The translation into collectable energy and hull loss is our arcade model.
      The candidate reaches and rates below are deliberately compressed design
      hypotheses, not measurements or a physical flux calculation. Their damage
      envelopes represent the ship’s protection being exceeded; orange light by
      itself does not establish how dangerous exposure is. Quiet is a chosen
      activity state, not a property promised by the letter K.
    </p>
    <h3>Where would the same approach put the ship?</h3>
    <p>
      Move the distance marker inward from 240 u through 160 u to 100 u. At 240
      u the dwarf supplies nothing, while the giant still offers a refill
      without radiation damage in this proposal. At 160 u the standard Sun
      remains free of radiation damage but the giant costs hull. At 100 u the
      giant’s body has already blocked the approach. Switch the main engine on
      to see which collection rates also pay for thrust.
    </p>
    <FieldComparison client:load />
    <h3>Accepted direction / Space to manoeuvre changes the encounter</h3>
    <p>
      Spatial variation alone is accepted as a worthwhile source of stellar
      encounter variety. The quiet dwarf in 02.1 has a distinct appeal: earning
      energy in a compact collection region asks for precise flying near a
      lethal body while gravity still matters. Its smaller manoeuvring space
      supplies the intended challenge. The giant instead offers room to remain
      in the collection field and fight approaching enemies. These support
      different ways of inhabiting an encounter, beyond differences in refill
      rate.
    </p>
    <p>
      The retained reference is the dwarf and giant pair shown in 02.1, with
      their exact working profiles available in the comparison’s disclosure. The
      accepted properties are the dwarf’s tight collection space and the giant’s
      broad collection space around a much larger body. Their numerical tuning,
      gravity profiles and eventual roster remain provisional. No flare mechanic
      is required to justify pursuing this spatial direction.
    </p>
    <p>
      The giant’s farther collection is an explicit candidate departure from the
      standard Sun’s shared collection/gravity boundary. A sustained
      hold-and-fight position is a possible encounter character. Whether it
      permits indefinite survival and score accumulation remains a gameplay
      hypothesis: this cross-section includes neither gravitational motion nor
      enemy pressure, weapon energy use or resource limits. The next comparison
      should investigate how the useful collection space relates to the pull and
      the ship’s room to manoeuvre, retaining these two spatial identities.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is a local field cross-section, not a flight experiment: gravity,
      escape, capacity and depletion are not evaluated. No gravity or friction
      values change. The next motion comparison must retain a named production
      configuration, identical Wasp fittings and starting reserves, and the same
      resource protection rules; analytical paths cannot stand in for
      resource-limited survival. New instrument support for candidate exposure
      and capacity feedback may be needed and remains an investigation item.
    </p>
    <p>
      Activity offers a separate kind of difference: a small cool star can flare
      violently, as observed on Proxima Centauri. A later comparison could test
      timing an approach between events instead of adding another steady radial
      profile. Advance warning and an arcade event cadence would be fictional
      design choices; neither is implied by the science or modelled here.
      <a
        href="https://science.nasa.gov/universe/exoplanets/neighboring-stars-bad-behavior-large-and-frequent-flares/"
        >NASA: Proxima’s observed flares</a
      >. Hotter stars and compact remnants remain possible alternatives, not
      commitments to expand this first comparison.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2 id="room-under-pull">02.2 / Collection space under the same pull</h2>
    <p>
      Keep the accepted tight dwarf and broad giant, and give all three stars
      the standard Sun’s production mass, cutoff and falloff. This isolates
      geometry: the same approach meets the same pull until body contact, while
      the available collection space changes. It is a controlled comparison, not
      a proposed claim that these stellar families have equal mass.
    </p>
    <p>
      With the main engine firing, the dwarf’s radiation-free net-positive
      collection span is narrow and wholly inside the well. The giant’s span
      extends beyond the well’s {gravityReach.toFixed(0)} u cutoff. That outer
      part permits collection with no stellar pull in this fixture: staying
      there may be an intentional fighting position, with its challenge coming
      from combat rather than gravity.
    </p>
    <table class="reference-table">
      <caption>
        Local collection spans / same Wasp, full forward thrust, zero spin
      </caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Encounter</th>
          <th>No exposure, positive net</th>
          <th>Radial width</th>
          <th>Width beyond gravity</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {reference.map(({ encounter, band }) => (
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">{encounter.name}</th>
            <td>
              {band.inner.toFixed(0)}–{band.outer.toFixed(0)} u
            </td>
            <td>{band.width.toFixed(0)} u</td>
            <td>
              {Math.max(
                0,
                band.outer - Math.max(band.inner, gravityReach),
              ).toFixed(0)}{" "}
              u
            </td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      These are centre-distance spans, not safe orbits or travel corridors. Net
      gain reaches zero at each outer limit; figures are rounded. The ship still
      needs to steer, and other burn states consume different amounts.
    </p>
    <h3>Repeat the same approach</h3>
    <p>
      The Motion instrument runs the same Wasp from x −400 u at three offsets:
      80, 160 and 260 u. Open the menu to switch stars or change starting speed;
      B1, B2 and B3 remain the shared input programs. The path pane shows the
      body, gravity boundary and collection boundary; the graphs connect
      distance and pull to net energy and accumulated energy change. A diamond
      ends a path at hull contact. Use Default scale when comparing space across
      stars.
    </p>
  </section>
  <InstrumentBand><MotionComparison client:load /></InstrumentBand>
  <section>
    <h3>B1 at 120 u/s / the starting comparison</h3>
    <p>
      These rows recalculate the opening configuration from current production
      definitions, independently of later menu edits. Collection and exposure
      are budgets over unlimited-power motion: capacity, depletion and hull loss
      do not feed back into a path. Negative energy change means reserve would
      be spent, not that a ship with an unspecified starting battery could
      finish the pass.
    </p>
    <table class="reference-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Encounter / offset</th>
          <th>Collection time</th>
          <th>Net energy</th>
          <th>Exposure budget</th>
          <th>End of trace</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {reference.flatMap(({ encounter, runs }) =>
          runs.map((run) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">
                {encounter.name} / {run.offset} u
              </th>
              <td>{run.collectionSeconds.toFixed(2)} s</td>
              <td>
                {run.energyChange > 0 ? "+" : ""}
                {run.energyChange.toFixed(2)} E
              </td>
              <td>{run.exposureBudget.toFixed(2)} hull</td>
              <td>
                {run.outcome} · {run.duration.toFixed(2)} s
              </td>
            </tr>
          )),
        )}
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      In the reference captured on 11 November 2026, the 80 u approach contacts
      the standard Sun but clears the dwarf with about 5.4 u of hull clearance,
      collecting for less than a second. The giant’s 160 u approach clears its
      body by about 7.5 u and incurs an analytical exposure budget of 1.86 hull;
      the standard Sun’s same approach has no exposure. Holding the pull fixed
      still changes both which passes fit and what they cost. The full 14 s
      energy budgets also include outbound flight after collection ends; they do
      not measure the value of staying near the star.
    </p>
    <details>
      <summary>Captured B1 reference / 11 November 2026</summary>
      <p>
        Same world scale, 0.2 px/u, centred on the star; cursor at 1.5 s.
        <a href="/studies/stellar-encounters-2026-11-11/022-dwarf.png"
          >Dwarf capture</a
        >,
        <a href="/studies/stellar-encounters-2026-11-11/022-sun.png"
          >standard Sun capture</a
        >,
        <a href="/studies/stellar-encounters-2026-11-11/022-giant.png"
          >giant capture</a
        >. The
        <a href="/studies/stellar-encounters-2026-11-11/022-reference.json"
          >reference conditions and results</a
        >
        retain the burn’s tick sequence, initial states, production
        configuration, source hashes and uncommitted working-tree qualification.
        The live instrument uses current production code; later changes can
        alter its results.
      </p>
    </details>
    <p>
      This round investigates how the accepted spatial characters behave under
      pull. It does not select final masses, prove indefinite survival or decide
      scoring balance. A resource-limited comparison with enemy pressure remains
      necessary to assess the giant’s sustained fighting position. Candidate
      exposure feedback is still a missing instrument capability; this round
      uses the existing analytical measurements without extending the
      instrument.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2 id="readiness">
      03 / Ready to build a trial with real resource limits
    </h2>
    <p>
      The accepted result is spatial variety: a tight collection region asks for
      precise flying, while a broad region permits staying and fighting. The
      initial delivery proposal is the standard Sun plus the quiet orange dwarf
      and cool giant from 02.1. Their exact profiles remain provisional. The
      motion comparison checks consequences under one gravitational profile; it
      supports taking the direction into a trial, without establishing sustained
      survival, score balance or final family tuning.
    </p>
    <h3>Give the trial coherent stellar identities</h3>
    <p>
      The dwarf should be less massive and less luminous than the standard Sun.
      A cool evolved giant can be much larger and more luminous while retaining
      roughly Sun-like mass; large size does not require a stronger well.
      <a
        href="https://science.nasa.gov/missions/hubble/goldilocks-stars-are-best-places-to-look-for-life/"
        >NASA: orange dwarfs</a
      >;
      <a href="https://www.eso.org/public/news/eso1741/"
        >ESO: a cool giant’s size and mass</a
      >. A proposed starting mass is 0.7 times the standard Sun for the dwarf
      and 1.0 for the giant. These are provisional game presets, not measured
      values or accepted tuning. Retain 02.1’s body and field profiles as the
      starting proposal, with the existing force law and cutoff. The equal-mass
      02.2 reference remains a separate controlled experiment.
    </p>
    <p>
      Temperature class and evolutionary family need separate identities: a cool
      giant is not a new letter in the spectral sequence. Colour continues to
      signal temperature; body size and the field boundaries communicate the
      spatial encounter. The existing procedural star renderer can supply the
      trial. Recognition at flight zoom still needs inspection before treating
      those cues as sufficient. Flares, new artwork and a complete stellar
      catalogue are outside this delivery proposal.
    </p>
    <h3>Reuse the laws; remove standard-Sun assumptions</h3>
    <table class="reference-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Existing system</th>
          <th>Adaptation required</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Collection and exposure</th>
          <td>
            The shared field schema and plateau/linear law already work.
            Production collection still takes its reach from gravity, and
            exposure still uses the standard Sun globally. Both must read the
            selected star’s independent fields, for stock and assembled ships
            and every affected actor.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Gravity and identity</th>
          <td>
            Production creates G and applies one tuned Sun mass. Resolve a
            named, validated profile before constructing the star; retain a
            separate family identity and supply its mass to the existing mass
            well. The old seven-class appearance table is not an adopted
            encounter catalogue.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">HUD and artwork</th>
          <td>
            The approved field renderer still assumes coincident collection and
            gravity boundaries and a 100 u hazard edge. Read all three actual
            reaches; apply the 2 CSS px inset only to coincident boundaries.
            Preserve line styles, active effects, collector scaling and terminal
            contact.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Trial and reproducibility</th>
          <td>
            The Suns Lab already embeds the real Simulation with battery
            capacity, depletion and applied damage. Configure that shared
            simulation through a validated profile identifier, rather than
            duplicating mechanics in a new study simulator. Retain the profile
            identity with the flight conditions used to reproduce a trial.
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Profile plumbing and a resource-limited Lab trial are feasible without a
      new physics system or a Motion instrument extension. The concrete blockers
      are the hardcoded fields, star selection and matching HUD geometry. Exact
      masses and rates can remain configurable trial values. The trial must
      revisit whether the lower-mass dwarf still rewards precise flying and how
      the giant behaves with finite capacity and real propulsion costs. Combat
      balance remains outside the evidence gathered here.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2 id="scope">04 / A shared profile model, then two trial encounters</h2>
    <p>
      <strong
        >No change to the standing experience-design principles is
        proposed.</strong
      >
    </p>
    <p>
      This is a proposed delivery scope for the accepted spatial direction. It
      leaves the study open and does not settle the roster or provisional
      masses. The first trial belongs in the existing Suns Lab; normal scored
      play and the decorative landing system retain the standard Sun. Random
      system selection, score-rule changes and travel remain separate decisions.
    </p>
    <ol class="delivery-slices">
      <li>
        <h3>Validated profiles / small–medium</h3>
        <p>
          Define the shared profile shape before changing consumers: stable
          identity, family and spectral class, body and appearance, mass, and
          independent collection/exposure fields. Keep named game presets in
          mode-a and generic schemas and field math in core. Default to the
          exact standard Sun; add the dwarf and giant as explicitly provisional
          trial profiles. Extend flight settings to carry a validated profile
          identity and retain the standard default for old settings.
        </p>
        <p>
          Owns: core Sun/field schemas, mode-a profile definitions and flight
          settings; star and gravity specs. Check invalid identities and field
          geometry, body/shoulder consistency, and standard-Sun value parity.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Simulation and field HUD / medium · depends on 1</h3>
        <p>
          Construct the chosen star once; route its mass and fields through
          production gravity, both charging paths, exposure and the world HUD.
          Keep inherent and fitted collection additive, capacity and starvation
          real, and practice’s resource protection explicit. Render separated
          and coincident boundaries from the same definitions that produce
          effects.
        </p>
        <p>
          Owns: core solar math, Physics and field renderer; mode-a Simulation
          and flight rendering; star, gravity and game-screen specs. Check field
          edges and shoulders, capacity, depletion, contact, applied exposure,
          actor parity and HUD geometry across zoom and display density. Prove
          that choosing the standard profile preserves its current behaviour.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Resource-limited Suns Lab trial / medium · depends on 2</h3>
        <p>
          Extend the existing Suns reference with a profile selector and
          repeatable starting conditions using the real Simulation. Show the
          current star, actual stored energy, hull and collection/damage rates.
          Compare the dwarf’s close manoeuvring and the giant’s broad collection
          space with identical Wasp fittings and reserves; retain the starting
          profiles and observed outcomes. Existing keyboard and landscape touch
          controls stay available in play.
        </p>
        <p>
          Owns: Suns Lab bench/article and its owning star spec. Inspect the Lab
          at 1280 px or wider, controls by keyboard, low reserves, full
          capacity, collecting during net drain and loss of hull. Update the
          article from current production energy costs; it still quotes the
          former Light ion draw. This slice establishes a concrete trial, not a
          difficulty rating.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      After the trial, retain or revise the provisional profiles according to
      their intended character. Wider stellar families and activity mechanics
      can build on the same model if they offer another pilot decision. A
      decision to enable stellar variety in scored play must separately define
      which encounters occur and how their differing survival opportunities
      belong in the score rules; that rollout is not hidden inside this patch.
    </p>
  </section>
</StudyLayout>

<style>
.delivery-slices {
  padding-left: var(--spacing-lg);
}
.delivery-slices li {
  margin-block: var(--spacing-xl);
}
.reference-table {
  width: 100%;
  border-collapse: collapse;
  margin-block: var(--spacing-lg);
  font-size: var(--font-size-label);
}
.reference-table caption {
  text-align: left;
  margin-bottom: var(--spacing-md);
  color: var(--color-text-muted);
}
.reference-table th,
.reference-table td {
  padding: var(--spacing-sm);
  text-align: left;
  vertical-align: top;
  border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);
}
.question-check {
  max-width: var(--measure-prose);
  margin-top: var(--spacing-lg);
  padding-top: var(--spacing-md);
  border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  color: var(--color-text-muted);
  font-size: var(--font-size-caption);
}
</style>
